p.src:
The first two classes can be anchored directly: $\begin{aligned}
\text{He pointed the gun at his friend.} &\sim SV\cdots,\\
\text{Way back across the yard, near the fence, where a tiny brook ran, she hid.} &\sim \cdots SV.\end{aligned}$ In the first, the kernel arrives immediately. The action shocks us at the front of the sentence, and the rest trails after as consequence. In the second, the modifiers delay the kernel. The reader waits. When she hid finally arrives, the sentence has hidden her grammatically before the scene reveals her physically.

